*Shell scripting:
--> shell scripting is simply a text file that consist set of commands that execute sequentially.
--> usually wriiten with .sh extension.


#!/bin/bash -----> Shebang line
# = sharp and 
! = bang

what is shebang line?
--> shebang refers to #!
-- in musical notation # called sharp and ! called bang
-- it is used to instruct the OS to use bash as a command interpreter.
-- shebang line specifing the interpreter.

*how to execute script:
1. ./<script_name>
eg: ./1.sh
--> if you are in the same directory as the script use relative path

2. /path/<script_name>
eg: /etc/1.sh
--> if you are in different directory, use absolute path

3. bash <script_name>
--> you are explicitly tell the shell to execute the script using spsecific interpreter. even if it does not have execute permission or a shebang line it will execute your code.

